WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:01.960
SPEAKER: Welcome back.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.560
In the last episode,
Kevin from GreenMobility

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.380
told us about
immutable deployments.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.570
So do immutable
deployments apply

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.250
to other components
of your system

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.500
as well besides
Cloud Run, Kevin?

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.840
KEVIN: Yeah, we want
to run jobs everywhere

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.612
in our production environment.

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:19.320
And for example, you
can get into trouble

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.870
with your cron jobs.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.040
Developers might add
or modify your jobs

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.350
in the cloud schedule,
and after a few weeks,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.170
you're not sure what jobs are
there and what they're for.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:28.770
SPEAKER: Ah.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.042
How do you prevent that drift
for Cloud Scheduler jobs?

00:00:32.042 --> 00:00:33.000
KEVIN: Let me show you.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.460
We're using Terraform to
deploy Cloud Scheduler jobs.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.530
And that way, all
job definitions

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.230
are in a Terraform manifest
file that's in source control.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.130
It's easy to see when a
job was added and why.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.590
Whenever we apply a
Terraform manifest,

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.950
Terraform compares the list of
scheduled jobs in the manifest

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.570
with the jobs in production.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.330
It then adds all the list of
jobs in production as needed

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.150
to make sure that production
contains only the jobs listed

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.650
in the manifest.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.730
SPEAKER: Nice.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.270
That ensures that the production
environment stays consistent.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.910
What else are you doing to
keep production under control?

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.850
KEVIN: We are also
focusing on security.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.040
We needed to provide
transparency and be

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.560
strict about who can see what.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.560
For example, access groups--
we have a BI and data.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:27.760
They can run reports.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.170
We have a DevOps group.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.310
They can deploy code
and view production.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.650
And then, we have a dev group.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.310
That can't view production,
but they can check in code.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.530
SPEAKER: Ah, but Kevin, if your
developers don't have access

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.180
to the production
environment, how

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.630
do they know what settings
and code would work there?

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.190
KEVIN: We have
many dev projects.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.440
Developers are free to
experiment with settings

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.690
and test their code
in those projects.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.690
And every now and then,
we destroy the dev

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.740
projects and create new
ones using the settings

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.900
in our production project.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:57.400
SPEAKER: Ah.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.320
As you went full
serverless, is there

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.560
anything that worked
differently than you expected?

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.635
KEVIN: Yeah, the
Cloud Scheduler.

00:02:03.635 --> 00:02:05.260
For example, it
wouldn't start new jobs

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.950
if old ones were running.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.830
So our solution
to get around that

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.080
was just using two
Cloud Schedulers.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.840
It's actually cool, because it
means that the scheduler keeps

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.550
track of what is
running, which can

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.520
prevent some really bad bugs.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.395
For example, if two
instances of the same job

00:02:19.395 --> 00:02:22.420
are trying to modify the
database at the same time,

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.790
for example, you could get
into really serious trouble.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.260
SPEAKER: Mm, that makes sense.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.620
KEVIN: Also, we find that many
of our jobs run at five minutes

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.600
past the hour, every hour.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.690
That puts a lot load on
our cloud SQL database.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.480
We could manually schedule the
job start times over the hour,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.083
but we have a lot of scheduled
jobs, and we keep adding more.

00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:43.000
It would be nice if Cloud
Scheduler could just

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.095
run our jobs randomly for us.

00:02:45.095 --> 00:02:46.720
SPEAKER: I will let
the project manager

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.520
for Cloud Scheduler know.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:49.990
Is there anything
else you've done

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.570
to be more productive
with Google Cloud?

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.550
KEVIN: Some tasks
I perform often,

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.680
like switching between
projects and users,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.620
requires a lot of clicking and
waiting in the Cloud Console.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.520
I wrote two command-line tools,
dpc-run and dpc-auth-user,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.610
to quickly navigate
between projects and users.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.260
They were easy to
write, as you can

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.460
do so much with the [INAUDIBLE]
commands from a shell script.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:13.438
Feel free to use it.

00:03:13.438 --> 00:03:14.980
SPEAKER: Nice, I
will check them out,

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.320
and I will put the link
in the description below.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.330
Any other learnings, Kevin?

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.280
KEVIN: We also need to
sync tens of thousands

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.730
of records between our system
and an external system,

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.350
and this happens a
few times per hour.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.200
At first, we had
a system dedicated

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.110
to running this, which
was slow and complicated.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.130
So what we did was
we split this big job

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:36.100
into 500 small jobs
using Cloud Tasks, which

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.630
meant we could run them
asynchronously in parallel.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.180
We found the Cloud Tasks made
it easy to kick off these 500

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.700
jobs quickly, and
also, the code that

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.820
ran each of these 500
small jobs became simpler.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.680
SPEAKER: 500 jobs?

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.580
Does it that make it
harder to handle errors?

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.420
KEVIN: It actually
makes it easier.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.460
Each job is shorter, so
fewer things can go wrong.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.190
And if any of the
500 jobs fails,

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.530
Cloud Tasks tries it again.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.220
We don't have to write any
of the retry logic ourselves.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.570
SPEAKER: Oh, nice.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.730
So Kevin, what's the verdict?

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:14.410
Does serverless get a thumbs
up or a thumbs down from you?

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.470
KEVIN: A big thumbs up.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.180
I could have built the data
just in backends, scheduled jobs

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.250
and so on using
virtual machines,

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.010
but our team has saved so much
time by doing it serverlessly

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.990
with Firestore Cloud Run,
BigQuery Cloud Task, and also

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:28.847
Cloud Scheduler.

00:04:28.847 --> 00:04:30.430
It means that we
have been able to add

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.940
more functionality to our
cloud sharing platform quicker.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.313
SPEAKER: Good to hear, Kevin.

00:04:35.313 --> 00:04:36.730
Thanks for sharing
your serverless

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.947
experiences, and thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:04:39.947 --> 00:04:41.530
If you have questions
for Kevin or me,

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.355
please add them in the comments.

00:04:43.355 --> 00:04:45.730
Also, this episode was a little
different from the others

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.800
in the Serverless
Expedition Series.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.870
Please let us know if
you found it useful,

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.140
and be sure to like this video
and subscribe to the channel.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:54.160
Until next time!

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

